_In Corollary 3 a pulley is substituted for the fixed point at the top of the plane, and in Corollary 4 the sphere on the plane is replaced by a column or cylinder with its axis perpendicular to the slant face of the plane._

==== Corollary 5

Let a perpendicular be drawn from the center of the column $D$ (@fig_12), such as $D K$, cutting the side of it at $L$; then the triangle $L D I$ will be similar to the triangle $A B C$, for the angles $C$ and $L I D$ are right-angles, $L D$ is parallel to $B C$, and $D I$ to $A B$; wherefore as $A B$ is to $B C$ so is $L D$ to $D I$; but as $A B$ is to $B C$ so is the weight of the column to the weight $E$, by the fourth corollary: therefore as $L D$ is to $D I$ so is the weight of the column to $E$: so that if in $K D$ we apply a lifting force $M$ in equilibrium with the column, it will be equal in weight to it by the fourteenth proposition: and finally as $L D$ is to $D I$ so is $M$ to $E$.

